      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "6e6b82bd-0c0c-54ab-a56d-795458d5d0e0",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Projects: Range Busters film series\n- Companies: Monogram Pictures\n- People: George W. Weeks, Brewster M. Higley\n- Places: Corriganville Movie Ranch, Smith County, Kansas, American West\n- Products: “Home on the Range”\n- Topics: Western films, folk songs\n\nFacts:\n- In 1872, Brewster M. Higley of Smith County, Kansas, originally wrote the lyrics of “Home on the Range” in a poem titled “My Western Home.”\n- “Home on the Range” is a classic western folk song that is sometimes called the unofficial anthem of the American West.\n- The Range Busters was a film series of 24 Westerns about the adventures of a trio of cowboys.\n- The Range Busters was produced by George W. Weeks and distributed by Monogram Pictures.\n- Many Range Busters films were shot at Corriganville Movie Ranch.\n- The Range Busters used “Home on the Range” as its theme song, and each film featured the heroes waving goodbye and promising to return in another adventure.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "9231eca5-2744-53b2-ac81-f08f0276e789",
        "text": "Page: Range Busters. Sentence: The Range Busters was a film series of 24 Westerns of the adventures of a trio of cowboys, many filmed at the Corriganville Movie Ranch, produced by George W. Weeks and distributed by Monogram Pictures.\nPage: Range Busters. Sentence: The series used \"Home on the Range\" as its theme song with each film featuring the heroes waving goodbye and promising to return in another adventure.\nPage: Home on the Range. Sentence: \"Home on the Range\" is a classic western folk song sometimes called the \"unofficial anthem\" of the American West.\nPage: Home on the Range. Sentence: The lyrics were originally written by Dr. Brewster M. Higley of Smith County, Kansas, in a poem entitled \"My Western Home\" in 1872.",
